Write the design document a person reviews the plan `{{ plan }}` as — terse prose, complete
interfaces — and store it as the project document `{{ native }}-design` of
`{{ plan }}`, rendered from the `design-doc` template:
`onepipeline template resolve design-doc --json | onetaskgraph document create ... --template-loader - --answers <file> --no-interactive`
for a new document, `onetaskgraph document render <id> --template-loader - --answers <file>`
for one that exists. `onetaskgraph template variables --template-loader -` over the same
resolution lists the answers it takes; the guidance comments in its chain say how to write
each.

{# llmlint: ignore-block[changed_behavior_has_e2e] A planner or design writer carries this out by its own reading of the store, which the suite never scripts a verdict for: it doubles the model's turn, so a journey could only assert what its own script chose. What the host decides around it is driven: the planning journeys hold that this step runs with this task, at its place in the flow, over the records the store holds. #}
- **Planned tasks.** Answer `graph` with exactly the JSON
  `onetaskgraph project graph {{ plan }} --format json --group-by orchestrator.unit` prints,
  and `delivers` with one short phrase per task id. When the plan has more than one task and
  a dependency edge, render the same graph's Mermaid text (the command without
  `--format json`) to a PNG with `just render-diagram <source.mmd> <image.png>`, answer
  `dag_image` with the image's file name and store it with `--asset <image.png>`.
- **Budgets.** Answer `plan_budgets` with the project's `orchestrator.plan-budgets` metadata
  and `budgets` with every task's `orchestrator.budgets` records, as the store holds them,
  each budget with its `node` and `location`, a version-3 changed budget's `previous` and
  the plan's `check_runtime_summary` kept inside them; invent none.
{# llmlint: ignore-end[changed_behavior_has_e2e] #}
- **Accepted opaque content.** Answer `accepted_opaque` from the project's
  `onepipeline.export_edges` metadata, verbatim, or `[]`.
- **Length.** Keep the stored body under 45,000 characters.

What the planner was asked to plan, for the document's What and Why — restate it as the
work's impact, never quote it or attribute it:

{{ brief_text }}

## Acceptance criteria

- The document is stored as the project document `{{ native }}-design` of `{{ plan }}`, rendered from the `design-doc` template with `onepipeline:design-doc` provenance, answering every variable it lists in the shape each describes.
- Its `graph` answer is what `onetaskgraph project graph {{ plan }} --format json --group-by orchestrator.unit` prints, and every image it references is an asset of the document.
- The dispatch reports where the store put the document.
